s I'm not with the elite  of the Republican Party Chris we've all  been watching the protests they continue  they were significant this weekend and  watch  and again do you think it's different  this time well that's you know the big  question I keep asking myself because  you know we're all veterans you and Gale  and Tony and me and we've seen this  story before whether it was Rodney King  or Eric garner Michael Brown in Ferguson  where you know there's a case of alleged  or in most cases confirmed undo police  force and protests some of them turning  into riots and calls for reform and  frankly nothing much happens and that's  the big question here and I don't have  been answered to it will things happen  the houses House Democrats are going to  propose a bill today that includes  things like more transparency some kind  of register for police who have  misconduct claims against them maybe  take away some of their qualified  immunity but will that get through  Congress and the Republican Senate I'm  not sure about that and I'm not sure how  much of a difference it will make so  we'll have to wait and see but being a  little bit of a skeptic I think it's  more likely that that we're gonna see  this story tragically again sometime in  a couple of years and say now we need to  make some changes one of the things I  was struck in in reading your book Chris  countdown 1945 and it reads like a  thriller by the way it really does cuz  you have you have the new president  Harry Truman coming into office and not  even realizing that an atomic bomb was  under development and suddenly facing  whether he should use it or not and and  one of the things that struck me is  there's an extraordinary amount of unity  in the government as they consider this  decision isn't there absolutely I mean  you know there as you're right  researching and then writing a book of  history you're always comparing that to  current times and one of the things that  really struck me was the extraordinary  unity we were in the middle of World War  two but whether it was the top military  the top scientists the top politicians  or just folks on the home front they  were all pulling together and one of the  astonishing facts is that for three  years a Manhattan Project was under way  a hundred and twenty five thousand  people at Oak Ridge Tennessee and out at  los alamos in New Mexico Hanford  Washington Wendover Utah and not a  single bit of the of the project broke  now obviously they didn't have the  internet or social media things that we  have today but it's they it was a secret  in the state a secret because everybody  was on the same team boy could we use  that now yeah and and and Chris Truman  was very methodical you point out about  his decision-making  he took counsel from everyone including  generals who disagreed correct yeah this  is one of the things that I really  appreciated about Truman he first of all  it was meticulous the choice was do we  invade Japan or do we use the atom bomb  maybe the biggest decision any president  had to make and he sought out advice  from everybody he has lunch just a few  days before the bomb has dropped with  Dwight Eisenhower the supreme Allied  commander Eisenhower says the Japanese  were already gonna surrender we  shouldn't be the first country to  unleash the bomb now Truman didn't  follow his advice but he didn't hate it  he didn't yell at him or throw him out  of the room he welcomed it he wanted the  best straightest advice from everybody  around to me but he said when I make the  decision you're back mate right Chris  Wallace it's a fascinating read thank  you so much for being with us this  morning countdown 1945 is on sale  tomorrow
 

